# The one root command the CI user on the Pi may run, and nothing else.
#
# The same line as `packaging/prod/deployci.sudoers`, on the other box, for the
# same reason: Tailscale SSH cannot restrict a command — the tailnet policy's
# `ssh` rules carry `action`, `src`, `dst`, `users`, `checkPeriod`, `acceptEnv`
# and `srcPosture`, and there is no `cmd` field — so an SSH grant is a shell
# grant, and `pi` on the Pi has passwordless sudo. Granting CI `root` or `pi`
# would mean "GitHub can be root on the Pi", so this is a user of its own whose
# only privilege is this line.
#
# There is deliberately **no argument wildcard**, and the trailing `""` is what
# says so: `/usr/local/sbin/selvage-pi-deploy ""` permits that one program and no
# argument at all, whereas the same line without the `""` would permit *any*
# argument, because an empty argument list in sudoers means "any" rather than
# "none". That distinction is the whole bound, and it is why the deploy request
# arrives on stdin.
#
# The script is root-owned mode 0755 under `/usr/local/sbin` (root:root 0755), so
# the user it is granted to cannot change what it does.
#
# Installs to `/etc/sudoers.d/selvage-deploy-pi`, root:root, mode 0440, and is
# read with `visudo -c -f` before it is put there. **Neither half of this is
# installed on the Pi today** — there is no `deployci` user on that box and no
# line in `/etc/sudoers.d` — which is part of why the deploy workflow cannot
# reach it; `README.md` beside this file owns that gap.
deployci ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/selvage-pi-deploy ""
